From 8868a48b9622f7c0aa8f9b400c85b91995e8f6c4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 03:19:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 06/14] feat(audit): record API writes with the principal in
 the write transaction

AuditLog now works on a shared Database like the outbox: put() writes a
record inside the caller's transaction without committing, record()
writes and commits one record on its own. Records gain key_id (the API
key id) and namespace; opening an existing database adds both columns.

SQLiteRegistry owns an AuditLog on its connection (audit_log property).
put, update_spec, delete and request_delete take a keyword-only
principal; when it is given, the same transaction that writes the
resource and its outbox event also writes an audit record with the
tenant, subject, key id and correlation id (shared with the event;
generated when missing), action create/update/delete, outcome success
and details linking the event id and resource version. A principal of
another tenant is rejected before anything is written, and an
update_spec call that changes nothing writes no record. Writes without a
principal are not audited: the controller's status writes (update_status
takes no principal), the instance check's among them, and the
reconciler's finalizer write-back and delete.

Principal gains key_id, set by the API key authenticator (None in dev
mode). The create, replace (PUT), patch (PATCH) and delete routes pass
the principal and the request's X-Correlation-Id to the registry, so a
PUT or PATCH that changes the service is recorded as action "update".
